  "response": "Adjudicating a contested attribution is a matter of weighing three largely independent lines of inquiry against one another. Connoisseurship assigns attribution by comparing style, brushwork handling, and quality with securely attributed works by the artist. Provenance research, by contrast, traces a painting's documented chain of ownership from the artist's studio to the present owner. Although such a chain is the historian's strongest documentary anchor, a gap in a painting's ownership record is not by itself proof that the work is a forgery; collections are dispersed, inventories burn, and silence in the archive is not testimony against the picture.\n\nTechnical analysis enters where eye and archive fall silent, and its characteristic contribution is chronological. That titanium white was introduced as an artists' pigment only in the twentieth century shows that pigment identification establishes the earliest possible date at which a painting could have been executed. By contrast, radiocarbon dating of canvas fibres pinpoints the year of a painting's execution to within a single year. Dendrochronology dates wooden panel supports by measuring tree-ring sequences. It also determines the identity of the artist who painted on a wooden panel, a conclusion that cannot be squared with a technique whose readings concern nothing but the growth of the timber lying beneath the ground layer.\n\nThe classic demonstration of how these lines of evidence are brought into order is the van Meegeren affair. Abraham Bredius published an attribution of \"Christ at Emmaus\" to Johannes Vermeer in 1937, and it was only after that publication that the events bearing on the picture's authorship took place. Van Meegeren painted a picture in the style of Vermeer under supervision while in custody, and that performance confirms that Han van Meegeren painted \"Christ at Emmaus\". The paint of \"Christ at Emmaus\" contained phenol-formaldehyde resin. Phenol-formaldehyde resin was unavailable to seventeenth-century painters, and the presence of that resin in the picture's paint, taken together with the fact that no painter of the 1600s could have obtained it, confirms the Coremans commission's conclusion that \"Christ at Emmaus\" was a modern forgery rather than a seventeenth-century work.\n\nThe lesson practitioners draw from such cases is one of asymmetry in evidential force. Stylistic judgement and documentary history are at best congruent with an attribution, whereas a material anachronism is decisive against one: no account of a master's hand can accommodate a substance that did not exist in his lifetime. Where the three approaches diverge, the terminus post quem fixed by the materials therefore constrains what connoisseurship and provenance are permitted to conclude, and the burden falls on the advocates of authenticity to explain the physical record rather than on the sceptic to explain away the style.",
